  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round1_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B022",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and development of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B003",
    "B007"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "Optical lattice clocks are a particularly clean example of a physical idea becoming a robust experimental regime. The magic-wavelength lattice-clock concept resolved a central limitation in precision spectroscopy, and Katori's conceptual invention together with Ye's decisive independent experimental development established a reproducible international platform. The resulting clocks have extraordinary agreement and enable stringent measurements of time, gravity, and fundamental physics; the achievement is mature without relying on speculative future applications.",
    "case_against": "The citation must remain tightly focused on optical lattice clocks rather than the broader optical-clock enterprise. We should also examine carefully whether the named division of credit properly distinguishes the original concept from the substantial enabling advances that made record performance possible.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "danielsson-ulf",
        "point": "Danielsson notes that lattice clocks have entered a new metrological regime but that their ultimate scientific discoveries beyond metrology are still developing.",
        "response": "That qualification is fair, but it does not weaken the central case. The prize-worthy result is the invention of a controllable measurement platform and its demonstrated precision, not a promise that a particular future fundamental discovery will occur. Its established ability to make previously inaccessible comparisons is already a major physical consequence."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "Olsson emphasizes both the exceptional reproducibility of lattice clocks and the need to draw the boundary between Katori's invention and the wider optical-clock field carefully.",
        "response": "I agree with that boundary condition and would make it explicit in the citation. Katori and Ye provide a compact attribution because the former is tied to the lattice-clock concept and the latter to defining experimental and metrological development; the committee should nevertheless undertake the historical attribution review before a final decision."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll stresses that optical lattice clocks have been replicated extensively and provide a durable platform for precision measurement, gravity, and fundamental tests.",
        "response": "This is the decisive maturity argument. Independent replication is especially important for an advance whose significance lies in the faithful control of many physical details; it shows that the achievement is a general physical method rather than an exceptional result from one laboratory."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "The historical division of credit between the original magic-wavelength lattice-clock concept and subsequent enabling experimental advances requires detailed review."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
